Subject: [PATCH 1/2] =?UTF-8?q?Phase=203=20NH15:=20wb=5Faltasmi=5Fparallel?=
 =?UTF-8?q?.v=20wrapper=20=E2=80=94=20latch-lock=20DV=20slot?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Gate latched_dataout load with !flag_data_valid so the slot acts as a
1-deep FIFO.  Previous behavior loaded on every am_data_valid pulse:
during a FAST_READ the megafunction's free-running gen_cntr streams
bytes at ~Mbyte/s, overwriting latched_dataout faster than firmware's
MMIO polling can consume — so firmware reads a non-deterministic byte
(whichever happened to be the most recent overwrite at the cycle
DATAOUT was read).  Root cause of the Phase C/D non-deterministic-
bytes finding (NH14 capture 646e1719: bytes vary per bitstream
rebuild AND per intra-boot consecutive read).

With this gate:
- First DV after a FAST_READ captures the byte; subsequent stream-
  overflow DV pulses are dropped (no overwrite).
- Firmware reads DATAOUT (dataout_read_req clears flag); next DV
  pulse then loads the next byte.

Paired firmware change: Phase E in mode_epcs_probe does ONE
CTRL=FAST_READ per byte (relying on NH10 auto-terminate after each)
with addr incremented per byte — gives deterministic per-address
reads at the cost of pre-amble overhead per byte (~30 sysclks).

NH14's earlier "addr→read_address linkage broken" framing is
REFUTED by this audit: read_address output port (epcs_ctrl.v line
1807) is the read_add_reg, whose enable (line 1159) fires only at
end_read_byte during a read op — it's a per-byte snapshot, never
updates in idle.  So *DP_ADDR writes never moving RADDR is expected
megafunction behavior, not a wrapper bug.

NH13 ctrl_reset stretcher (patch 0020) remains as defensive: it
still correctly resets read_add_cntr to 0 between ops (verified in
NH14 capture, C-pre-addr RADDR=0x0).

Refuted but kept for reference: SHIFT_BYTES is a write-only signal
(shift_bytes_wire only in lpm_counter pgwr_data_cntr clk_en line
1591 and scfifo4 wrreq line 1694).  Firmware's CTRL.b4 pulse during
reads has no effect — bytes shift in via gen_cntr autonomously.
Phase E drops the SHIFT_BYTES bit entirely.

For multi-byte burst reads (Phase 6 mode 't' SHA verify), the
1-deep slot still loses bytes that arrive while the slot is full —
intentional.  Phase 9-class redesign would add a wrapper FIFO +
length counter; out of scope for this iteration.
---
 rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v | 27 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v b/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v
index 673075f..3f1a31a 100644
--- a/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v
+++ b/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v
@@ -194,7 +194,32 @@ module wb_altasmi_parallel (
         end else begin
             if (am_illegal_erase) flag_illegal_erase <= 1'b1;
             if (am_illegal_write) flag_illegal_write <= 1'b1;
-            if (am_data_valid) begin
+            // NH15 (2026-05-17 night): only load latched_dataout when the
+            // slot is empty (flag_data_valid=0).  Previous behavior loaded
+            // on EVERY am_data_valid pulse, so during a FAST_READ the
+            // megafunction's free-running gen_cntr produced ~Mbyte/s of DV
+            // pulses that overwrote latched_dataout faster than firmware's
+            // MMIO polling could consume it — firmware ended up reading a
+            // non-deterministic byte (whichever happened to be the most
+            // recent overwrite at the cycle DATAOUT was read).  Root cause
+            // of Phase C/D non-deterministic-bytes finding (NH14 capture
+            // 646e1719 — bytes vary per bitstream rebuild AND per intra-
+            // boot consecutive read).
+            //
+            // With this gate, the slot is a 1-deep FIFO: first DV pulse
+            // captures the byte, subsequent DV pulses are dropped, firmware
+            // reads DATAOUT to consume (dataout_read_req clears flag), and
+            // the very next DV pulse can load the next byte.  Paired
+            // firmware change: ONE CTRL=FAST_READ per byte (relying on
+            // NH10 auto-terminate after each), with addr incremented per
+            // byte — Phase E in mode_epcs_probe.
+            //
+            // For burst reads (>1 byte per op), the slot's 1-deep depth
+            // still loses any bytes that arrive while the slot is full —
+            // intentional, since for multi-byte bursts firmware can't keep
+            // up anyway.  Phase 9-class redesign would add a wrapper FIFO
+            // + length counter; out of scope for this iteration.
+            if (am_data_valid && !flag_data_valid) begin
                 flag_data_valid <= 1'b1;
                 latched_dataout <= am_dataout;
             end else if (dataout_read_req) begin
